# Rust Type System and Serialization

Lux ships a pure-Elixir type system for exchanging structured data with Rust.
It models the Elixir <-> Rust boundary on top of the same JSON-shaped wire
format that Rust's [`serde`](https://serde.rs) ecosystem produces, so Elixir and
Rust can share `struct`/`enum` definitions and round-trip complex values without
a native build step.

The public entry point is `Lux.Rust.Types`.

## Supported type mappings

| Rust type            | Elixir representation                          |
| -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| `bool`               | boolean                                        |
| `i8`..`i128`, `u8`..`u128` | integer (range validated)                |
| `f32`/`f64`          | float                                          |
| `String`/`&str`      | binary (UTF-8 validated)                       |
| `char`               | single code point binary                       |
| `Option<T>`          | `nil` or the wrapped value                     |
| `Vec<T>`/slices      | list                                           |
| `(A, B)`             | tuple (encoded as a JSON array)                |
| `HashMap<K, V>`      | map with string keys                           |
| `struct`             | map or Elixir struct (`__struct__` dropped)    |
| `enum`               | atom (unit variant) or `{variant, payload}`    |

## Encoding and decoding

```elixir
iex> alias Lux.Rust.Types
iex> Types.encode(%{x: 1.0, y: 2.0}, {:struct, [x: :f64, y: :f64]})
{:ok, %{"x" => 1.0, "y" => 2.0}}

iex> alias Lux.Rust.Types
iex> Types.decode(%{"Ok" => 7}, {:enum, [ok: :i64, err: :string]})
{:ok, {:ok, 7}}
```

All functions return `{:ok, value}` or `{:error, reason}` where `reason` is a
typed tuple such as `{:integer_out_of_range, 300}` or `{:missing_field, :y}`.

## Custom type definitions

Named types bundle a Rust name with the shape needed to serialize and generate
code:

```elixir
iex> alias Lux.Rust.Types
iex> point = Types.define("Point", {:struct, [x: :f64, y: :f64]})
iex> Types.encode(%{x: 1.0, y: 2.0}, point)
{:ok, %{"x" => 1.0, "y" => 2.0}}
iex> Types.rust_type(point)
{:ok, "Point"}
```

## Generating Rust source

`Lux.Rust.Types.SchemaGenerator` emits idiomatic Rust definitions annotated with
`#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]`:

```elixir
iex> alias Lux.Rust.Types
iex> Types.generate({:enum, [red: :unit, green: :unit, custom: {:tuple, [:u8, :u8, :u8]}]}, name: "Color")
{:ok,
 "#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub enum Color {\n    Red,\n    Green,\n    Custom((u8, u8, u8)),\n}"}
```

## Complex conversions

Nested structs, enums, options, vectors, tuples and maps compose freely:

```elixir
iex> alias Lux.Rust.Types
iex> type = {:struct,
...>   id: :u64,
...>   tags: {:vec, :string},
...>   location: {:option, {:tuple, [:f64, :f64]}},
...>   metadata: {:map, :string, :string}
...> }
iex> value = %{id: 1, tags: ["a", "b"], location: {51.5, -0.1}, metadata: %{"k" => "v"}}
iex> Types.encode(value, type)
{:ok,
 %{
   "id" => 1,
   "location" => [51.5, -0.1],
   "metadata" => %{"k" => "v"},
   "tags" => ["a", "b"]
 }}
```

## Serialization safety

Decoding never interns untrusted wire strings into atoms. Object keys are
preserved as strings, and only enum variants that are explicitly declared in a
type specification are lowered to atoms.
